1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,433
You need to get a file to another server. Email is not an option.

2
00:00:04,453 --> 00:00:09,297
The safe way is built into every Linux system, and it takes one short command.

3
00:00:09,767 --> 00:00:13,246
In this video: securely transfer files between systems.

4
00:00:17,766 --> 00:00:20,349
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:20,819 --> 00:00:23,295
Securely transfer files between systems.

6
00:00:23,765 --> 00:00:29,102
We'll copy a file with scp, copy a folder, synchronize it with rsync,

7
00:00:29,122 --> 00:00:30,422
and look at sftp.

8
00:00:39,942 --> 00:00:44,562
All three tools, scp, rsync and sftp, use ssh underneath.

9
00:00:44,582 --> 00:00:50,994
So everything is encrypted on the way, and you log in the same way as with ssh.

10
00:00:59,464 --> 00:01:02,839
The pattern is always: tool, source, destination.

11
00:01:02,859 --> 00:01:06,996
A file on another system is written user at host colon path.

12
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:20,498
One honest note: our lab has a single machine.

13
00:01:20,518 --> 00:01:24,279
So localhost, the machine itself, plays the other system.

14
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:29,521
The ssh connection and the commands are exactly the same as with a real server.

15
00:01:35,041 --> 00:01:37,134
First we create a small file.

16
00:01:37,154 --> 00:01:43,036
echo writes the text, and the greater-than sign sends it into the file report dot

17
00:01:43,056 --> 00:01:46,242
txt. ls dash l confirms it: seventeen bytes.

18
00:01:55,762 --> 00:01:59,424
Now scp. Source: report dot txt. Destination:

19
00:01:59,444 --> 00:02:04,087
student at localhost, colon, slash tmp slash. That means:

20
00:02:04,107 --> 00:02:10,959
put it in the folder slash tmp of the other system. It prints nothing when it works.

21
00:02:10,979 --> 00:02:13,086
ls shows the file arrived.

22
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:32,918
To fetch a file, swap the order: the remote file first, a local name second.

23
00:02:32,938 --> 00:02:37,733
Then cmp compares the two files. No output means they are identical.

24
00:02:51,253 --> 00:02:55,387
Now a folder. We make the folder project, with two small files in it.

25
00:02:55,407 --> 00:02:56,652
One command per line.

26
00:03:03,122 --> 00:03:08,273
scp dash r copies a folder with everything inside, r for recursive.

27
00:03:08,293 --> 00:03:14,371
And ssh can run one command on the other system: here, ls on the copied folder.

28
00:03:14,391 --> 00:03:15,838
Both files arrived.

29
00:03:31,358 --> 00:03:37,421
scp copies everything every time. rsync is smarter: it only sends what changed.

30
00:03:37,441 --> 00:03:39,807
First, is rsync installed? Yes.

31
00:03:44,277 --> 00:03:45,267
rsync dash a v:

32
00:03:45,287 --> 00:03:50,652
a is archive mode, which keeps folders, permissions and times, and v is verbose,

33
00:03:50,672 --> 00:03:54,421
so it lists what it sends. Note the slash after project:

34
00:03:54,441 --> 00:03:58,999
it means the contents of the folder. The first run sends both files.

35
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:22,585
Now we add a third file, and run the same rsync again. Look: only c dot txt is sent.

36
00:04:22,605 --> 00:04:28,019
The other two are already there. That is why rsync is the tool for backups.

37
00:04:43,539 --> 00:04:47,601
The third tool is sftp, an interactive file transfer session.

38
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:51,549
Here we feed it one command through a pipe: ls on our file.

39
00:04:51,569 --> 00:04:55,764
In a normal session you type get to download and put to upload.

40
00:05:09,284 --> 00:05:12,720
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

41
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:17,741
We reboot, and the copy in our home folder is still there.

42
00:05:17,761 --> 00:05:21,687
The files we transferred are ordinary files, kept like any other.

43
00:05:31,207 --> 00:05:38,169
Your turn. With scp, copy exam dot txt to slash tmp slash exam dash copy dot txt.

44
00:05:38,189 --> 00:05:44,374
With rsync, back up the folder docs to slash tmp slash docs dash backup.

45
00:05:44,394 --> 00:05:46,184
Then run the checker.

46
00:05:52,654 --> 00:05:54,405
Try before you open the solution.

47
00:05:59,925 --> 00:06:04,150
Next up: list, create, and delete partitions on GPT disks.

48
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:07,523
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
